                <h2>Professional Journey</h2>

                <h3 class="journey-phase">Construction &amp; Inspection (2006–2016)</h3>
                <p>
                    I started at WSDOT as a Transportation Technician 1 apprentice, completing a 2,000-hour
                    program to earn Journeyman status. Over the next decade I progressed through automatic
                    promotion steps (TT1 &rarr; TT3), then earned a Transportation Engineer 2 position in a
                    new office where I ran my first complete solo structural job. I became the go-to inspector
                    for structural projects — lead inspector on multiple new bridge constructions and retaining
                    wall projects. On a large I-5 corridor project in the Centralia/Chehalis area covering
                    approximately $20–30 million in structural work, I sole-managed over $1 million in Force
                    Account work on a Stage 1 project while simultaneously performing structural inspections.
                    Built strong working relationships with project superintendents and foremen, two of whom
                    offered me jobs.
                </p>
                <p>
                    When I transitioned from TE2 to BE2, I earned my Bridge Inspector certification and
                    completed a week-long production inspection tour across Washington State, including UBIT
                    truck bucket operations. This field experience — physically standing on the bridges,
                    understanding how they're built and where they fail — is something you can't get from
                    a database.
                </p>

                <h3 class="journey-phase">The Pivot to Software (2014–2017)</h3>
                <p>
                    While inspecting bridges by day, I started teaching myself to code at night. From 2014–2016,
                    I joined open-source Android communities (BlissRoms, XDA), maintaining custom ROM builds and
                    coordinating remote builds on dedicated Linux servers. That hands-on tinkering — plus
                    building Arduino projects and custom PCs — taught me how software and hardware systems
                    work from the ground up. I applied for a Bridge Engineer 2 position with the Bridge
                    Preservation office, taking on SQL and coding tasks alongside preservation duties. I was
                    enrolled in a BE2-to-BE3 training program and a work-week degree program for software
                    development.
                </p>

                <h3 class="journey-phase">Database &amp; Application Specialist (2017–Present)</h3>
                <p>
                    When a Bridge Engineer 4 / Database &amp; Application Specialist position opened in
                    Bridge Asset Management, I was encouraged to apply — and made the jump directly from
                    BE2 to BE4, skipping BE3 entirely. Since 2017, I've been the primary developer
                    and database specialist for WSDOT's bridge data systems, working largely independently.
                </p>
                <p>
                    With years of daily AASHTOWare BrM experience — bridge inventory management, inspection
                    workflows, and ETL development — I've seen firsthand how bridge management software impacts
                    engineering decisions across our 8,400+ structure inventory. That combination of field
                    inspection experience and development skills gives me a perspective most developers
                    don't have — I know what bridge engineers actually need from their tools because I've been one.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Currently, I'm leading the migration of multiple legacy Microsoft Access applications —
                    including the Bridge Condition Report and Seismic Retrofit Status Module — to a modern
                    Blazor Server web front-end backed by SQL Server. This consolidates fragmented bridge
                    data systems into a unified Structure Asset Management (SAM) platform serving 50+
                    engineers and inspectors statewide. I manage my own DevOps environment — sprint planning,
                    epic and issue creation, delivery plans, CI/CD pipelines, and commits tied to work items.
                </p>
                <p>
                    My experience spans the full lifecycle: field inspection, data collection, database design,
                    application development, reporting, and decision support. I've recovered 8+ years of unlogged
                    batch records via Python/pdfplumber extraction, remediated broken database objects and data
                    across disparate legacy systems, and built
                    comprehensive documentation where none existed. I regularly train team members on new systems
                    and work directly with bridge inspectors and project engineers to translate their needs into
                    working software.
                </p>
                <p>
                    I also developed and maintained the ETL pipelines that feed AASHTOWare BrM. When
                    BrM 7 was released, I built a complete set of new ETLs using SQL Server Integration
                    Services and SQL Agent jobs to transform data from the Bridge Preservation Office's
                    custom inspection database into the BrM schema — reverse-engineering their entire
                    database system to ensure proper insertion with correct referential integrity across
                    multiple tables. I automated these pipelines across dev, test, and production environments,
                    and created a suite of ETLs that could rapidly stand up new BrM 7 instances with all
                    agency data and customizations — enabling fast environment provisioning for testing
                    and version upgrades.
                </p>

                <h3 class="journey-phase">Entrepreneurship &amp; AI (2012–Present)</h3>
                <p>
                    The entrepreneurial thread runs through my entire career. From 2012–2022, I co-owned
                    a portrait photography business — shooting weddings, family sessions, and managing large
                    contracts for local youth sports leagues of 20+ teams. I handled the technical side:
                    lighting, editing (Photoshop, Lightroom), client delivery workflows, and business operations.
                    My high school sports photography was published in a local newspaper during a contracted period.
                    That decade of running a small business — managing clients, timelines, budgets, and quality —
                    shaped how I approach every project.
                </p>
                <p>
                    More recently, I founded NW OpsBridge Automations — a consulting business bringing
                    automation and AI tools to trades and service businesses. I built the entire operation
                    from scratch: LLC registration, website, scheduling, payment processing, and AI-powered
                    lead generation tools. I stay current with cutting-edge AI — using Claude Code for
                    personal development workflows, experienced with GitHub Copilot, and not beholden to
                    any single model or provider. For me, tokens are tokens and I'll use whatever tools
                    help me ship the best work.
                </p>

